  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study serves as a proof-of-concept and does not validate the modeled psychoacoustic annoyance against actual behavioral or psychological responses from the children in the study areas.",
      "what": "This study introduces a spatial analysis method that models children's exposure to traffic noise on school commutes by integrating psychoacoustic annoyance as a perceptual metric into pedestrian isochrone networks. The approach was tested across catchments for 18 primary schools in metropolitan Melbourne, Australia, calculating a spatial 'Amenity Cost' to map walk-quality from a receiver-centric perspective.",
      "why": "It shifts the evaluation of pedestrian infrastructure from objective noise emission levels to human psychological perception, allowing urban planners to visually identify quiet, child-friendly routes and test the impact of noise-mitigation interventions."
    }
